# DESCRIPTION
# Using seagulls, set up a hypothesis test for a sample mean difference.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Nursing-Med Calc')
## DBsection('Problem')
## KEYWORDS('null hypothesis', 'alternative hypothesis', 'two-sample test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", 'PGML.pl', "parserPopUp.pl", "PGcourse.pl");

Context("Numeric");

@opts = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$dd1  = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$dd2  = DropDown([@opts], 1, useHTMLSelect => 0);

$n = random(10, 20);
$x = random(50, 90, 0.1);

$sig_level = list_random(1, 2, 5, 10);
$pval      = random(1, 99) * list_random(0.001, 0.0001, 0.00001);
$dd3       = DropDown(
    [
        "We will reject the null hypothesis.",
        "We fail to reject the null hypothesis."
    ],
    $pval < $sig_level / 100 ? 0 : 1
);

BEGIN_PGML

Researchers were interested in whether seagulls are less likely to steal food 
when a human is looking at them vs when they are not being looked at. For each 
of [$n] seagulls, they measured how long it took the seagull to approach a bag of 
French fries when a human was looking at them and how long it took that same 
seagull to approach the fries when it was not being looked at. The researchers 
found a mean difference (looked at - not looked at) of [$x] seconds. They do a 
hypothesis test at a [$sig_level]% level of significance.

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd1} [_]{Real(0)}

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd2}  [_]{Real(0)}

c) What is [`n`]? [_]{$n}

d) What is the alpha level? [`\alpha = `] [_]{Real($sig_level/100)}

e) What is [`\bar{x}`]? [_]{Real($x) }

f) If the p-value is [$pval], what should be our conclusion? [_]{$dd3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Since the mean [`\mu`] is interpreted as the difference between then, the null hypothesis is the neutral relationship and that is [`\mu=0`] (there is no difference in their ratings.)

b) Since the researchers want to know if their ratings are different, the alternative
hypothesis is [`\mu \neq 0 `].

c) [`n`] is the number of samples  or [`[$n]`].

d) The alpha level is the significance level as a fraction or [`\alpha = [$sig_level/100]`].

d) The sample mean difference is [`\bar{x} = [$x]`].

e) Since the [`p`]-value is [@ $pval < $sig_level/100 ? 'smaller' : 'greater'@] than
    the level of significant of [`\alpha = [$sig_level/100]`], converted to a 
    fraction, the reserchers should 
    *[@ $pval < $sig_level/100 ? '' : 'not '@]reject the null hypothesis*.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
